//   becomes vacuous. In the adaptive/manual constrained branch, either
//   AdaptiveConstrainedStep returns eosToken and we break, or it returns a
//   parser-valid next token; after optional context-based boosting we still
//   mask with MaskValidNextAndEos before sampling, so any non-EOS sampled token
//   is valid and AppendConstrainedToken preserves parser validity.
// suffix: In the unconstrained branch, when we open a span we set
//   currentConstrainedOut := [], and the length-0 suffix of generated matches
//   []. In the close branch, CloseConstrainedSpan appends the closing delimiter
//   and resets currentConstrainedOut := [], so the implication is vacuous. In
//   the constrained append branches, AppendConstrainedToken appends the same
//   token to generated and currentConstrainedOut, preserving the suffix
//   equality directly.
// cost accounting: We use a local `steps` counter and increment it exactly once
//   for each cost-bumping helper/sampling action: UnconstrainedStep,
//   CloseConstrainedSpan, AdaptiveConstrainedStep, or manual masked sampling
//   with ChooseNextToken. Query-only context updates (ExtractAfterKeyword,
//   FlattenTokenGroups, IntersectTokenSets, GroupContaining) do not change
//   `steps`, and `cost` remains 0 inside the loop and is assigned `steps` once
//   at the end, so returned cost <= maxSteps.
// progress: UnconstrainedStep and CloseConstrainedSpan each append at most one
//   token and we do steps := steps + 1. In the adaptive constrained branch,
//   either we break on EOS or append exactly one constrained token and still
//   increment steps by 1. In the manual masked-sampling branch, sampling also
//   consumes one step; on EOS we break, otherwise AppendConstrainedToken adds
